\p{Field note from the Neuromodulation Conference (NYC, 2026), filed under [[conf-neuromod-nyc-2026]]. A second Bikson-group \strong{Wearable Disposable Electrotherapy (WDE)} device — this one a self-powered bandage that accelerates wound healing. Not a brain device, but the clearest example at the conference of a \strong{fully self-contained, electronics-free, printed} stimulation patch, which is why it sits in the device-application category.}

\image{ccny-wde-wound.jpg}

\subtree{\title{Device architecture}
\taxon{remark}
\p{A printed stack, all in a thin bandage: (1) skin bandage/adhesive, (2) flexible PET sheet, (3) printed battery (printed carbon / anode / separator / cathode), (4) stimulation electrode (printed silver anode + cathode), (5) ion-conductive hydrogel, (6) wound dressing. Self-powered, electronics-free, no external stimulator or wires, no electrode contact with the wound bed, single-use controlled dose, scalable, bandage-like.}
}

\subtree{\title{Performance and results}
\taxon{finding}
\p{Target 300 µA; WDE current \strong{286 ± 98 µA}; 120 min/day; WDE charge 2.06 ± 0.71 C vs CC 2.16 C. In a full-thickness rat wound model, WDE matched conventional constant-current electrotherapy and \strong{accelerated healing by 29\%} (50\% closure at 7.10 ± 1.36 vs 5.02 ± 1.35 days, p = 0.003), with earlier exudate resolution and ~25\% higher tissue cellularity (p = 0.016). Histology showed greater collagen deposition, angiogenesis, and macrophage response; cellular-resolution BEM-FMM modeling linked local fields (>0.44 V/m at tissue interfaces, ~0.30 V/m near wound edges) to the effect.}
\p{Funding: NIH UG3NS134619, R01EB035129, UG3DA048502, UH3DA048502 (bioRxiv preprint).}
}

\p{Why it matters for us: proof that a useful therapeutic dose fits in a printed, disposable, self-powered patch — the extreme low-cost, electronics-free end of the device design space that [[bcf-devices]] surveys.}
